Document Intelligence Pipeline (BankOCR / PrimeOCR)
The document intelligence pipeline illustrates how Spenta converts probabilistic AI outputs into deterministic enterprise processes [3, 4]. Implemented for financial institutions such as Prime Bank, the system parses unstructured handwritten and printed financial records through a self-hosted, eight-step asynchronous workflow [2, 3, 5].


Documents are uploaded to an S3-compatible, on-premises storage repository [3, 4]. The system applies automated image preprocessing, including deskewing, binarization, and contrast enhancement, to maximize character legibility [3, 4]. Primary visual extraction is executed by a self-hosted LLaMA 3.2 Vision model [3, 4, 5]. If character ambiguity is detected, the pipeline routes data to deterministic fallbacks, including Tesseract OCR, LayoutLMv3, and spaCy named-entity recognition [3, 4, 5].


Field-level validation rules and confidence scores are calculated automatically [3, 4]. Extracted data meeting or exceeding a 0.88 confidence score is written directly to core banking databases [3, 4]. Any field falling below the 0.88 threshold is flagged and queued for human operator review [3, 4]. Once validated, the transaction record is committed, and an immutable log entry is generated for audit compliance [2, 3, 4].


This hybrid approach guarantees data integrity while eliminating manual processing overhead [3, 4]. Turnaround times dropped from 17.5 minutes per form to under 30 seconds, maintaining over 95% accuracy for handwritten entries and generating a 450% three-year ROI for financial clients [2, 3, 5].
Governed Agentic AI ("Autopilot Suggests, Captain Signs")
To minimize operational risk in enterprise deployments, Spenta enforces a clear governance principle: "The autopilot suggests; the operator signs" [2, 4]. Autonomous reasoning loops evaluate operational contexts, parse documents, detect anomalies, and generate recommendations [3, 4]. However, the system requires explicit human authorization before executing consequential or irreversible actions [2, 4].


Automated AI agent tasks include searching policy document repositories, drafting shift change summaries, categorizing incoming paperwork, identifying roster anomalies, and suggesting schedule optimizations [2, 4]. Conversely, actions restricted to human authorization include modifying clinical treatment plans, committing core banking database entries, finalizing staff schedules, authorizing prescriptions, and approving regulatory filings [2, 4]. This operational boundary enables high-volume automation across routine tasks while preserving human accountability and regulatory compliance [2, 4].


